WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.769
-Our first guest tonight
is a legendary actor

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:05.638
you know from his work in films
"The Thomas Crown Affair,"

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:06.940
the "Mamma Mia!" movies,

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.308
"Mrs. Doubtfire,"
and as James Bond.

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.043
He stars in "Black Adam,"

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:14.280
which premieres in theaters
and IMAX October 21st.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.083
Let's take a look.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.551
-A wise man once told me

00:00:18.551 --> 00:00:20.887
a bad plan is better
than no plan at all.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:24.257
-That was before
we had a very bad plan.

00:00:24.257 --> 00:00:26.292
-Let it go.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:28.961
I'd rather be fighting with him
than against him.

00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:31.397
-What's the helmet telling you?

00:00:31.397 --> 00:00:34.233
Somebody's gonna die.

00:00:34.233 --> 00:00:36.903
Who is it, Atom Smasher?
It's Atom Smasher.

00:00:36.903 --> 00:00:39.405
-Surprisingly no.

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.474
-Is it me?

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:45.278
-When it's time for you and I
to say goodbye, you'll know.

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:48.548
-Please welcome to the show
Pierce Brosnan, everyone.

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:50.383
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:58.691 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:06.999 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:06.999 --> 00:01:15.307 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:16.810
That was very kind of you.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:18.444
I feel like not enough
of our guests

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:19.846
do a little dance
right at the end.

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:22.415
-Oh, I just saw it
on the TV one night.

00:01:22.415 --> 00:01:24.684
And I thought I'd do it now.

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:26.953
-Very natural. Very good.
-Very natural.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.188
-I'm so happy to have you here.
-Thank you.

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:32.024
-We worked together before
but never met.

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:34.293
-This is true --
Sarah Jessica Parker, yourself.

00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:38.097
-We were in a film called
"I Don't Know How She Does It."

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:40.733
And you were the second lead.

00:01:40.733 --> 00:01:42.168
And then I was on the end

00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:43.803
where they had to
make the arrow different --

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.771
from a different angle.

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:47.072
-Yeah.
I remember that.

00:01:47.072 --> 00:01:49.275
It was winter here.
It was a beautiful time.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.909
-It was nice.
It was working in New York City.

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:52.278
Always a pleasure.
-I enjoy it.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:54.514
I always love New York.
It's so good to be back here.

00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:55.948
-It's great to be back here.

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:59.752
This is -- Did you know
this world, the Black Adam --

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:01.387
You play Doctor Fate
in the film.

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:04.390
These are famous
DC Comic characters,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.792
maybe not the most famous,
though.

00:02:05.792 --> 00:02:08.427
-My sons told me about them.

00:02:08.427 --> 00:02:10.863
And so I did know
about Black Adam.

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:12.999
I did know about Doctor Fate.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:15.701
And, you know, it was
the last thing I expected

00:02:15.701 --> 00:02:18.204
but quietly desired

00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:20.673
and wondered whether
I would ever be asked

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:21.941
to be in one of these movies.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.043
And it just came out
of left field.

00:02:24.043 --> 00:02:25.945
-So you finally found your way
into a superhero movie.

00:02:25.945 --> 00:02:28.614
Very nicely done.
-I did, yeah, really.

00:02:28.614 --> 00:02:31.383
Well, slow and steady.
Tonight's a big night.

00:02:31.383 --> 00:02:33.386
And it's a great film.

00:02:33.386 --> 00:02:36.089
It's a beast of a film.
It's a feast of a film.

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:38.491
And it was amazing
being with Dwayne

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:39.892
and watching him do his thing.

00:02:39.892 --> 00:02:41.827
I've watched this man rise

00:02:41.827 --> 00:02:45.198
to the beautiful prominence
that he has now as an actor.

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.766
And the cast were wonderful.

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:50.369
Aldis Hodge and Quintessa
and Noah.

00:02:50.369 --> 00:02:52.705
We game a kind of
little quartet,

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:54.107
you know, the Justice Society.

00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:56.675
-One of the very important
things about Doctor Fate,

00:02:56.675 --> 00:02:59.311
which I'm happy to report
they got right, is the helmet,

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:00.880
which is --
-Oh, yes.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.348
-They did a beautiful job.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:06.519
Were you involved at all
in the forging of the steel

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:07.820
to make the helmet or --

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:10.723
-No, but Jaume Collet-Serra,
who's the director,

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:13.259
who's a really fantastic
director, brilliant man,

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:15.661
and greatest admiration
for him, again,

00:03:15.661 --> 00:03:18.398
because this was a beast
of a film, he said,

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:22.501
"Do you want to have a hand in
creating this beautiful helmet?"

00:03:22.501 --> 00:03:24.403
And I said, "Sure."
And we talked about it.

00:03:24.403 --> 00:03:26.506
And sent me photographs.

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:28.107
I said,
"That looks like a bucket."

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:32.611
You know, it was just --
And something sleek and elegant.

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:34.747
And so, you know, in the movie,

00:03:34.747 --> 00:03:37.250
you see me
carrying this helmet around.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.852
And that was the aesthetic,
beautiful one.

00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:43.388
And then they had another one
which really was like a bucket.

00:03:43.388 --> 00:03:45.224
So it fit on my head.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:46.525
-It was -- Oh, I see.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.460
So there's the prop you carry
and then -- Yeah.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:49.928
-Yeah.
-Yeah, 'cause it's too sleek

00:03:49.928 --> 00:03:51.731
to actually fit
on a human head, right?

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:53.266
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:55.267
-I will say, it would have
been very funny,

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.236
for all your wonderful acting

00:03:57.236 --> 00:03:59.606
and all the seriousness
of your dialogue,

00:03:59.606 --> 00:04:01.507
if then you would just
put a full bucket

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.476
on your head at the end.
-Exactly.

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:05.311
Oh, I'm sure someone will do it.

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:07.179
You'll see it at some point.

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:09.616
-Were you aware --
Growing up in Ireland,

00:04:09.616 --> 00:04:11.183
what were the superheroes
you were aware of

00:04:11.183 --> 00:04:12.919
back in the days of your youth?

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:16.689
-Oh, well, I grew up in a small
country town, Southern Ireland.

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:18.924
And there were two cinemas,
the Palace, the Lyric.

00:04:18.924 --> 00:04:22.228
And I grew up on a kind of diet
of cowboy movies.

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:24.263
-Sure.
-My mother, bless her,

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:25.731
would send me, from London,

00:04:25.731 --> 00:04:28.300
comic books,
the "Eagle" comic books.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:32.871
Um, I guess Batman was my hero.

00:04:32.871 --> 00:04:36.475
So, you know, as a little boy
in Ireland, Christian Brothers,

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:38.377
we'd put our raincoats
around our neck

00:04:38.377 --> 00:04:41.747
and swing from the bicycle shed,
you know.

00:04:41.747 --> 00:04:44.717
-A very -- Well, an upgrade on
costume, then, this time, yeah.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.051
-Very much so.

00:04:46.051 --> 00:04:47.420
-Even a bucket would have been
an upgrade over that.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:48.521
-True.

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:50.255
-You have been --

00:04:50.255 --> 00:04:53.559
You've been an artist for almost
as long as you've been an actor.

00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:56.061
And you actually have
a gallery show coming up.

00:04:56.061 --> 00:04:58.397
This is some of your
beautiful artwork here.

00:04:58.397 --> 00:05:01.133
-Thank you for showing --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:04.270
Thank you.
-I'm wondering --

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.639
It's --
I guess it's a little similar

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.974
in that when you make a film,
you know, you do it

00:05:08.974 --> 00:05:12.845
and then a great deal of time
later, people get to judge it.

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:14.113
For a gallery show,
it's the same.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:15.781
Do you get nervous
before people come in

00:05:15.781 --> 00:05:17.249
to see one of your shows?
-Yes.

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:20.052
I mean, this has on the cards
for a few years now.

00:05:20.052 --> 00:05:22.422
I've been painting since '87.

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:23.923
-Wow.

00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:26.425
-I started as
a trainee commercial artist

00:05:26.425 --> 00:05:30.162
and discovered acting and became
an actor, thank heavens.

00:05:30.162 --> 00:05:33.031
These paintings --
Anthony Bourdain.

00:05:33.031 --> 00:05:35.835
Broke my heart the morning
I heard the news.

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:38.170
And I went and painted it
straightaway.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.206
The other one
is called "Night Marcher."

00:05:40.206 --> 00:05:44.009
We live out there in Hawaii,
so it has a certain influence.

00:05:44.009 --> 00:05:47.547
And, you know, I've just accrued
so many paintings,

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:49.381
and my wife, Keely, said,
"You got to show.

00:05:49.381 --> 00:05:51.117
You have to show."
-Is that her way of being like,

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:52.218
"You got to get them
out of the house"?

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:53.819
-Got to get them
out of the house.

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:56.222
Too much storage, money.

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:57.790
Our life is in storage.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:06:02.327
So May 13th, we're gonna
have a show in L.A.

00:06:02.327 --> 00:06:05.398
I'll -- On May 16th, I turn 70.

00:06:05.398 --> 00:06:07.299
-Alright.
Look at that.

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:08.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:08.734 --> 00:06:10.636
-Get in under the wire.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:13.572
-So I -- We talked a little bit
backstage.

00:06:13.572 --> 00:06:15.240
This was -- I did not realize.

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:16.875
This is one of my favorite
films, "The Long Good Friday."

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:18.144
-It's a great movie.
It's a great movie.

00:06:18.144 --> 00:06:20.446
Holds up incredibly well.
You're in it.

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:22.514
I did not realize
it was your first film,

00:06:22.514 --> 00:06:25.284
as evidenced by the fact
you appear on the poster

00:06:25.284 --> 00:06:27.619
less than I appeared on
"I Don't Know How She Does It."

00:06:27.619 --> 00:06:28.988
So...
-Yeah.

00:06:28.988 --> 00:06:31.189
-That is not saying a lot
about your billing at the time.

00:06:31.189 --> 00:06:32.558
There's a wonderful --

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:33.726
It's the final scene
in the film.

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:34.827
I don't want to
give anything away.

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:36.428
But it's you
and the great Bob Hoskins.

00:06:36.428 --> 00:06:37.363
-That's right.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:38.498
-He gets in the back seat
of a car.

00:06:38.498 --> 00:06:39.631
You turn around with a gun.

00:06:39.631 --> 00:06:41.734
It's an incredibly dramatic
final scene.

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:43.335
-Yeah.
-He was --

00:06:43.335 --> 00:06:45.671
It was like you and I on
"I Don't Know How She Does It."

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:47.707
You never met him.
-Never saw the man.

00:06:47.707 --> 00:06:50.076
I was out of drama school
maybe a year.

00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:52.878
My director said, "You've got
a movie, 'Long Good Friday.'

00:06:52.878 --> 00:06:55.781
You're playing
IRA Man Number One."

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:57.483
I went, "Okay. Great.

00:06:57.483 --> 00:07:00.318
I'm in the movies.
I'm off to the races."

00:07:00.318 --> 00:07:02.487
And they just said --

00:07:02.487 --> 00:07:04.056
They didn't send me the script.

00:07:04.056 --> 00:07:07.126
They said, "Show up
at Tooting Bec swimming pool

00:07:07.126 --> 00:07:09.028
6:00 tomorrow morning."

00:07:09.028 --> 00:07:11.964
I went and I was
IRA Man Number One.

00:07:11.964 --> 00:07:13.799
-Yeah.

00:07:13.799 --> 00:07:15.434
Got to do a murder right away.

00:07:15.434 --> 00:07:17.103
-Got to kill him --
pick a guy up

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:19.305
and then stab him in the shower.

00:07:19.305 --> 00:07:20.339
[ Laughter ]

00:07:20.339 --> 00:07:21.841
Usual fare.

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:24.009
But it's a -- Yes.

00:07:24.009 --> 00:07:25.744
I mean, the soundtrack
on this movie --

00:07:25.744 --> 00:07:27.646
-It's one of the most iconic.
It's amazing.

00:07:27.646 --> 00:07:29.648
I watched the scene today,

00:07:29.648 --> 00:07:31.984
and every time you watch
anything from the film,

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:33.986
you remember exactly
how haunting that score is.

00:07:33.986 --> 00:07:38.023
-Yeah, and the end of the movie,
they --

00:07:38.023 --> 00:07:41.861
we got to the Savoy Hotel, and I
really wanted to drive the car,

00:07:41.861 --> 00:07:43.395
because I knew
what the scene was now,

00:07:43.395 --> 00:07:46.365
that we had the guy, we had
Bob Hoskins, number-one guy.

00:07:46.365 --> 00:07:48.501
And I thought, "I really want
to drive the car.

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:50.369
I just so want to drive the car.

00:07:50.369 --> 00:07:53.105
Gonna look like Steve McQueen.
It's gonna be so cool."

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:54.706
And the director,
John Mackenzie, said,

00:07:54.706 --> 00:07:55.841
"Right,
you're in the front seat.

00:07:55.841 --> 00:07:57.409
In the front.
Get down there now."

00:07:57.409 --> 00:07:59.612
And I thought, "Oh [bleep]
This is no good.

00:07:59.612 --> 00:08:01.013
This is really boring."

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:05.050
And we drove down the Strand,
and John Mackenzie,

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:07.119
the director, God bless him,
he drove.

00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:09.588
Phil Meheux was in the back
with the camera.

00:08:09.588 --> 00:08:11.189
And he said, "Action."

00:08:11.189 --> 00:08:13.459
And he said, "Just come up
and point the gun."

00:08:13.459 --> 00:08:14.794
And Bob Hoskins wasn't there.

00:08:14.794 --> 00:08:16.695
He was just, you know,
a dot on the camera.

00:08:16.695 --> 00:08:19.064
-I mean, you certainly would
never watch it and not think --

00:08:19.064 --> 00:08:22.101
As actors, you both obviously
do an incredible job

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:24.103
making me believe you were there
at the same time.

00:08:24.103 --> 00:08:25.537
-Well, it's Bob Hoskins.

00:08:25.537 --> 00:08:27.840
Bob Hoskins
just owns that scene.

00:08:27.840 --> 00:08:30.176
And I just happened
to be part of that scene.

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:31.610
-Yeah, I guess probably still,
like,

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:32.812
that's less of a special effect

00:08:32.812 --> 00:08:34.580
than we're gonna see
in "Black Adam."

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.350
-"Black Adam" is huge.
It's a colossal piece.

00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:40.786
-I think it speaks to exactly

00:08:40.786 --> 00:08:42.754
how long you've been
in this business

00:08:42.754 --> 00:08:45.424
that you go from IRA Man
Number One to Doctor Fate.

00:08:45.424 --> 00:08:46.525
Not a bad ride.

00:08:46.525 --> 00:08:48.461
-Stay in the ring
as long as you can.

00:08:48.461 --> 00:08:50.630
Thanks a million.
-Pierce Brosnan, everybody.

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:53.231
"Black Adam" -- theaters
and IMAX October 21st.

00:08:53.231 --> 00:08:55.067
We'll be right back
with Sharon Horgan.

